daughters may have different interaction cross sections with the pions which are the predominant hadrons in the medium. Thus the relative yields of the resonances destroyed by the daughter particles' re-scattering effect should be different for different resonances. On the other hand, the amount of resonance signals re-produced by the re-generation effect in the hadron medium should also be different due to the same reason. Considering the above discussions, the differences between the observed resonances yields and their corresponding primordial yields might be different for different resonances, such as \(K^{*0}\), \(\rho^{0}\), \(\phi\), \(\Delta^{++}\), etc.

